        "id": 1297255
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-16T22:11:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Jean, this field is a __u64 in the Linux kernel. Although java now supports \"long\" data type to represent an unsigned 64-bit long, I feel using BigInteger should cause less confusion. Thoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "547fbad1_8b5e1734",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 144,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 144,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "ab3f3dc860097a264965df9d3fbd1c0045667aa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c447e62f_0fc3388e",
        "filename": "framework-t/api/current.txt",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 144,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-17T06:22:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Personally I would favor a long. BigInteger carries a huge cost both in memory footprint and performance compared to a long, and I don\u0027t think we\u0027ll need to exceed a long in 5G or even 6G land. When I mentioned this to Lorenzo and Maze they also thought it was unnecessary.\n\nAt most, you may want to cap it to Long.MAX_VALUE if it exceeds it (java longs are signed, as opposed to __u64) but I don\u0027t think you really risk transmitting 10 exabytes any time soon.",
      "parentUuid": "6b1ca584_184bcea0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 144,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 144,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "ab3f3dc860097a264965df9d3fbd1c0045667aa7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
